course: dictionary
lesson: 9105
title: Many meanings of smart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so check the words around it before you choose one. «When you are stuck, calling a grown-up is the smart thing to do.» Here smart means clever and sensible, because it is a good choice when you need help.
explain: «A smart meter shows how much energy the house is using right now and what it costs.» A meter is a machine, so smart must describe a machine that uses a computer and the internet. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Smart can also mean neat and well-dressed, as in «a smart uniform» for photo day. Smart can even mean to sting, as in «my eyes smarted from the smoke». Look at the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: Smart means clever and quick to learn and understand.
fact: Smart can mean neat, tidy and well-dressed, as in «a smart uniform».
fact: A smart device uses a computer and the internet, like a smartphone or a smart speaker.
fact: To smart means to sting or hurt sharply, as in «my eyes smarted».
fact: In a story, «a smart answer» may mean a rude, cheeky reply, so the context matters.
exercise: «When you are stuck, calling a grown-up is the smart thing to do.» What does smart mean here? | A phone you carry in your pocket | To sting or hurt sharply | Neat and well-dressed | *Clever and sensible
exercise: «A smart meter shows how much energy the house is using right now.» What does smart describe in this sentence? | Something that stings | Neat and tidy | Clever and quick to learn | *A machine that uses a computer and the internet
exercise: «Smart shoppers read the label, not the box.» Find and copy one word that shows the shoppers are sensible. | *smart | box | label | shoppers
test: «My eyes smarted from the smoke.» What does smarted mean? | Glowed brightly in the dark | Felt very clever | *To sting or hurt sharply | Looked neat and tidy
test: Which meaning of smart fits a smart speaker in your home? | Quick to learn new things | *A device that uses a computer and the internet | To sting or hurt | Neat and tidy
exam: In a story, Ben says, «a smart answer» to his teacher. What might this mean if the answer is cheeky? | A neat, tidy reply | *A rude, cheeky reply | A stinging, painful reply | Clever and quick to learn
